{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"A gastrointestinal carcinoid tumor is cancer that forms in the lining of the gastrointestinal tract.","source_span":"A gastrointestinal carcinoid tumor is cancer that forms in the lining of the gastrointestinal tract."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"The GI tract is made up of the stomach, small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum), colon, and rectum.","source_span":"Stomach.    -  Small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, and ileum).    -  Colon.    -  Rectum."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"GI carcinoid tumors form from neuroendocrine cells scattered throughout the chest and abdomen, most found in the GI tract.","source_span":"Gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors form from a certain type of neuroendocrine cell (a type of cell that is like a nerve cell and a hormone-making cell). These cells are scattered throughout the chest and abdomen but most are found in the GI tract."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"GI carcinoid tumors are rare and most grow very slowly, occurring mostly in the small intestine, rectum, and appendix.","source_span":"GI carcinoid tumors are rare and most grow very slowly. Most of them occur in the small intestine, rectum, and appendix."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Carcinoid syndrome may occur if the tumor spreads to the liver or other parts of the body, or if tumor cells enter the blood.","source_span":"Carcinoid syndrome may occur if the tumor spreads to the liver or other parts of the body...This can also happen if tumor cells enter the blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of carcinoid syndrome include redness/warmth in the face and neck, abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, wheezing or trouble breathing, and fast heartbeat.","source_span":"Redness or a feeling of warmth in the face and neck. - Abdominal pain. - Feeling bloated. - Diarrhea. - Wheezing or other trouble breathing. - Fast heartbeat."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Risk factors for GI carcinoid tumors include having a family history of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome or neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) syndrome.","source_span":"Having a family history of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome or neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Risk factors for GI carcinoid tumors also include conditions affecting stomach acid production, such as atrophic gastritis, pernicious anemia, or Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","source_span":"Having certain conditions that affect the stomach's ability to make stomach acid, such as atrophic gastritis, pernicious anemia, or Zollinger-Ellison syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Some GI carcinoid tumors, especially of the stomach or appendix, cause no signs or symptoms and are often found during tests or treatments for other conditions.","source_span":"Some tumors, especially tumors of the stomach or appendix, may not cause signs or symptoms. Carcinoid tumors are often found during tests or treatments for other conditions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of GI carcinoid tumors in the rectum include blood in the stool, pain in the rectum, and constipation.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of GI carcinoid tumors in the rectum may include the following: -  Blood in the stool.       - Pain in the rectum.       -  Constipation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[0,2,3],"question":"What is a gastrointestinal carcinoid tumor and where do these tumors typically occur?","answer":"It is cancer that forms in the lining of the gastrointestinal tract, arising from neuroendocrine cells; most GI carcinoid tumors are rare, slow-growing, and occur in the small intestine, rectum, and appendix."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which organs make up the gastrointestinal tract according to the document?","answer":"The stomach, small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, and ileum), colon, and rectum."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"Under what circumstances can carcinoid syndrome occur, and what are its signs and symptoms?","answer":"It can occur if the tumor spreads to the liver or other parts of the body, or if tumor cells enter the blood. Symptoms include facial/neck redness or warmth, abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, wheezing or trouble breathing, and a fast heartbeat."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What family history conditions increase a person's risk of gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors?","answer":"A family history of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome or neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) syndrome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What stomach-acid-related conditions are listed as risk factors for GI carcinoid tumors?","answer":"Atrophic gastritis, pernicious anemia, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome — conditions that affect the stomach's ability to make stomach acid."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000010_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What symptoms might suggest a carcinoid tumor located in the rectum?","answer":"Blood in the stool, pain in the rectum, and constipation."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"Myeloproliferative neoplasms are a group of diseases in which the bone marrow makes too many red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets.","source_span":"Myeloproliferative neoplasms are a group of diseases in which the bone marrow makes too many red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"There are 6 types of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms.","source_span":"There are 6 types of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"The 6 types of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms are chronic myelogenous leukemia, polycythemia vera, primary myelofibrosis (also called chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis), essential thrombocythemia, chronic neutrophilic leukemia, and chronic eosinophilic leukemia.","source_span":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia.    -  Polycythemia vera.    -  Primary myelofibrosis (also called chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis).     -  Essential thrombocythemia.    -  Chronic neutrophilic leukemia.    -  Chronic eosinophilic leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"Chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms sometimes become acute leukemia, in which too many abnormal white blood cells are made.","source_span":"Chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms sometimes become acute leukemia, in which too many abnormal white blood cells are made."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"There is no standard staging system for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms.","source_span":"There is no standard staging system for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"A JAK2 gene mutation is often found in patients with polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, or primary myelofibrosis.","source_span":"A JAK2 gene mutation is often found in patients with polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, or primary myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"MPL or CALR gene mutations are found in patients with essential thrombocythemia or primary myelofibrosis.","source_span":"MPL or CALR gene mutations are found in patients with essential thrombocythemia or primary myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"There are eleven types of standard treatment for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms, including watchful waiting, phlebotomy, platelet apheresis, transfusion therapy, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, other drug therapy, surgery, biologic therapy, targeted therapy, and high-dose chemotherapy with stem cell transplant.","source_span":"Eleven types of standard treatment are used:          - Watchful waiting     -  Phlebotomy      -  Platelet apheresis      - Transfusion therapy     -  Chemotherapy      -  Radiation therapy      - Other drug therapy     -  Surgery      - Biologic therapy      - Targeted therapy     - High-dose chemotherapy with stem cell transplant"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","statement":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy involves removing bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone.","source_span":"The removal of bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What happens in myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"The bone marrow makes too many red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"How many types of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms are there, and what are they?","answer":"There are 6 types: chronic myelogenous leukemia, polycythemia vera, primary myelofibrosis (chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis), essential thrombocythemia, chronic neutrophilic leukemia, and chronic eosinophilic leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Is there a standard staging system for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"No, there is no standard staging system; treatment is instead based on the specific type of myeloproliferative neoplasm the patient has."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which gene mutation is often found in patients with polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, or primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"A JAK2 gene mutation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In which conditions are MPL or CALR gene mutations found?","answer":"In patients with essential thrombocythemia or primary myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How many types of standard treatment exist for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms, and what are some examples?","answer":"Eleven types, including watchful waiting, phlebotomy, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, surgery, biologic therapy, targeted therapy, and high-dose chemotherapy with stem cell transplant."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"Polycythemia vera is a disease in which too many red blood cells are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Polycythemia vera is a disease in which too many red blood cells are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"In patients older than 65 years or with a history of blood clots, the risk of stroke or heart attack from polycythemia vera is higher.","source_span":"In patients who are older than 65 years or who have a history of blood clots, the risk of stroke or heart attack is higher."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"Patients with polycythemia vera have an increased risk of acute myeloid leukemia or primary myelofibrosis.","source_span":"Patients also have an increased risk of acute myeloid leukemia or primary myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"Symptoms of polycythemia vera include headaches and a feeling of fullness below the ribs on the left side.","source_span":"Symptoms of polycythemia vera include headaches and a feeling of fullness below the ribs on the left side."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"Other symptoms of polycythemia vera include double vision or seeing dark/blind spots, itching all over the body especially after warm or hot water, a reddened face, weakness, dizziness, and unexplained weight loss.","source_span":"Double vision or seeing dark or blind spots that come and go.    - Itching all over the body, especially after being in warm or hot water.    - Reddened face that looks like a blush or sunburn.    - Weakness.    - Dizziness.    - Weight loss for no known reason."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"A serum erythropoietin test is used to diagnose polycythemia vera.","source_span":"a serum erythropoietin test is used to diagnose polycythemia vera"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"In polycythemia vera, the erythropoietin level is lower than normal because the body does not need to make more red blood cells.","source_span":"In polycythemia vera, the erythropoietin level would be lower than normal because the body does not need to make more red blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","statement":"Treatment of polycythemia vera may include phlebotomy, chemotherapy with or without phlebotomy, biologic therapy using interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha, and low-dose aspirin.","source_span":"Phlebotomy.     -  Chemotherapy with or without phlebotomy.     -  Biologic therapy using interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha.     - Low-dose aspirin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is polycythemia vera?","answer":"A disease in which too many red blood cells are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which patients with polycythemia vera have a higher risk of stroke or heart attack?","answer":"Patients older than 65 years or those with a history of blood clots."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What are the main symptoms of polycythemia vera?","answer":"Headaches and a feeling of fullness or pressure below the ribs on the left side; other symptoms include double vision or dark/blind spots, itching (especially after warm water), a reddened face, weakness, dizziness, and unexplained weight loss."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"How is polycythemia vera diagnosed, and what does the key blood test show?","answer":"A serum erythropoietin test is used; in polycythemia vera the erythropoietin level is lower than normal because the body doesn't need to make more red blood cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What other cancer risks are increased in patients with polycythemia vera?","answer":"An increased risk of acute myeloid leukemia or primary myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What treatments may be used for polycythemia vera?","answer":"Phlebotomy, chemotherapy with or without phlebotomy, biologic therapy using interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha, and low-dose aspirin."}
